### What is the squaring a sorted array problem?

Given a sorted array that may contain negative numbers, return a new array of the squares of each number, also sorted. The complication is that a large negative squared can be bigger than a small positive squared, so naively squaring left to right does not preserve the sorted order.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class SquareSortedArray {

    public static int[] sortedSquares(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        int[] result = new int[n];
        int left = 0, right = n - 1;
        int pos = n - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int leftAbs = Math.abs(arr[left]);
            int rightAbs = Math.abs(arr[right]);

            if (leftAbs > rightAbs) {
                result[pos] = leftAbs * leftAbs;
                left++;
            } else {
                result[pos] = rightAbs * rightAbs;
                right--;
            }
            pos--;
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] pnl = {-7, -3, 2, 5, 8};
        System.out.println("Sorted squares: " + Arrays.toString(sortedSquares(pnl)));
    }
}
```

```text
Sorted squares: [4, 9, 25, 49, 64]
```

### Why do the largest squares sit at the ends?

In a sorted array with negatives, the most negative value sits at the far left and the most positive sits at the far right. Their absolute values are the largest in the array, so their squares are the largest. The smallest absolute value, and thus the smallest square, sits somewhere in the middle near the transition from negative to positive.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class EndMagnitudes {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {-10, -5, -2, 1, 3, 8};
        System.out.println("Left end magnitude: " + Math.abs(arr[0]));
        System.out.println("Right end magnitude: " + Math.abs(arr[arr.length - 1]));
        System.out.println("Smallest magnitude near middle: " + Math.abs(arr[3]));
    }
}
```

```text
Left end magnitude: 10
Right end magnitude: 8
Smallest magnitude near middle: 1
```

### How is filling from the back different from filling from the front?

Filling from the back means placing the largest square first and working down. This naturally produces a sorted result because at each step the largest remaining square is always at one of the two ends. Filling from the front would require finding the smallest square first, which is somewhere in the middle and harder to locate efficiently.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class FillDirection {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {-7, -3, 2, 5, 8};
        int n = arr.length;
        int[] result = new int[n];
        int left = 0, right = n - 1, pos = n - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            System.out.println("left=" + left + "(" + arr[left] + ") right=" + right
                    + "(" + arr[right] + ") → filling pos=" + pos);
            int lAbs = Math.abs(arr[left]), rAbs = Math.abs(arr[right]);
            if (lAbs > rAbs) { result[pos] = lAbs * lAbs; left++; }
            else { result[pos] = rAbs * rAbs; right--; }
            pos--;
        }
        System.out.println("Result: " + Arrays.toString(result));
    }
}
```

```text
left=0(-7) right=4(8) → filling pos=4
left=0(-7) right=3(5) → filling pos=3
left=1(-3) right=3(5) → filling pos=2
left=1(-3) right=2(2) → filling pos=1
left=2(2) right=2(2) → filling pos=0
Result: [4, 9, 25, 49, 64]
```

### Why is the two-pointer approach better than sort-after-square?

Squaring every element then sorting costs O(n log n). The two-pointer approach is O(n) because it exploits the existing sorted order. For large arrays this difference matters.

| Approach | Time | Space | Preserves order? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Square then sort | O(n log n) | O(1) in-place sort | No, needs re-sort |
| Two-pointer fill-from-back | O(n) | O(n) for result | Yes |

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class NaiveVsTwoPointer {

    public static int[] naiveApproach(int[] arr) {
        int[] result = new int[arr.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            result[i] = arr[i] * arr[i];
        }
        Arrays.sort(result);  // O(n log n)
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {-7, -3, 2, 5, 8};
        System.out.println("Naive: " + Arrays.toString(naiveApproach(arr)));
    }
}
```

```text
Naive: [4, 9, 25, 49, 64]
```

### What about integer overflow?

Squaring large values can overflow a 32-bit integer. For example, squaring 50,000 gives 2,500,000,000 which exceeds `Integer.MAX_VALUE`. Use `long` for the square or cast before multiplying.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class SquareOverflow {

    public static long[] sortedSquaresLong(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        long[] result = new long[n];
        int left = 0, right = n - 1, pos = n - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            long lAbs = Math.abs((long) arr[left]);
            long rAbs = Math.abs((long) arr[right]);
            if (lAbs > rAbs) { result[pos] = lAbs * lAbs; left++; }
            else { result[pos] = rAbs * rAbs; right--; }
            pos--;
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] large = {-50000, -30000, 20000, 40000};
        System.out.println("Long squares: " + Arrays.toString(sortedSquaresLong(large)));
    }
}
```

```text
Long squares: [400000000, 900000000, 1600000000, 2500000000]
```

### What if all elements are non-negative?

If the array has no negatives, squaring preserves the sorted order and a simple left-to-right pass is sufficient. The two-pointer approach still works correctly in this case, but the left pointer never moves because the right end always has the larger magnitude.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class AllPositive {

    public static int[] simpleSquare(int[] arr) {
        int[] result = new int[arr.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            result[i] = arr[i] * arr[i];
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        System.out.println("Simple: " + Arrays.toString(simpleSquare(arr)));
    }
}
```

```text
Simple: [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single-element array, all negatives, all zeros, and an array with one negative and one positive.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class SquareEdgeCases {

    public static int[] sortedSquares(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        int[] result = new int[n];
        int left = 0, right = n - 1, pos = n - 1;
        while (left <= right) {
            int lAbs = Math.abs(arr[left]), rAbs = Math.abs(arr[right]);
            if (lAbs > rAbs) { result[pos] = lAbs * lAbs; left++; }
            else { result[pos] = rAbs * rAbs; right--; }
            pos--;
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Single: " + Arrays.toString(sortedSquares(new int[]{-3})));
        System.out.println("All neg: " + Arrays.toString(sortedSquares(new int[]{-5, -3, -1})));
        System.out.println("All zero: " + Arrays.toString(sortedSquares(new int[]{0, 0, 0})));
        System.out.println("Mixed: " + Arrays.toString(sortedSquares(new int[]{-2, 3})));
    }
}
```

```text
Single: [9]
All neg: [1, 9, 25]
All zero: [0, 0, 0]
Mixed: [4, 9]
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should explain why the largest squares sit at the ends rather than just stating the algorithm. They should also mention the overflow risk and the O(n) versus O(n log n) distinction. In production financial code at the National Bank of India, squared magnitudes of P&L figures are used for variance and volatility calculations, and using `BigDecimal` for precision is the standard practice.
